= Teamwork

== Team formation

Our team consists of six members with complementary backgrounds. Arianna specialises in Artificial Intelligence, Matteo, Sara and Elisa in Data Science, and Alessia in Human-Computer Interaction, all within Computer Science. Emilio studied Engineering Physics and is now specialising in High-Performance Computing.

These skills supported the main areas of ComplAI, including regulatory data processing, AI architecture, user experience, and scalability. Some team members had also previously founded a startup, giving the group direct experience of the time, budget, and legal constraints faced by early-stage ventures.

== Working process

Our first idea was not ComplAI: we started from a blockchain-based crypto transaction monitoring concept (see the #link(<sec-ideation>)[Ideation] section for details on why it did not survive contact with the market). Testing it against real interviews and a feasibility analysis showed the actual pain point was elsewhere: it was not that transaction data could not be trusted, but that companies could not keep up with regulation in the first place. That realisation pushed us to pivot towards ComplAI, moving from a niche security tool to a broader, more urgent concern shared across AI-driven products. The pivot was uncomfortable, since it meant dropping weeks of work, but it set the tone for how we worked from then on: we treated evidence from the market as more important than staying loyal to our first idea.

Once we had ComplAI as our direction, things did not fall into place feature by feature. We kept going back and forth between what the product should promise, what we could charge for it, and who would actually pay for it, and we changed our minds on all three more than once. By the time we got to the landing page and the pitch deck, most of that back-and-forth had already happened behind the scenes: what looks like a finished value proposition on paper is really the result of several earlier versions we scrapped.

Work was split up based on who was best suited for what, but we tried not to let that turn into six people working in isolation. We checked in with each other regularly, said when something was not working instead of pushing it through, and kept adjusting as we understood the problem better.

== Individual contributions

Roles were never assigned on paper, but looking back on the process that built this report and the subsequent pitch, each of us ended up covering a fairly consistent part of the work.

Sara acted as the Boss: when we got stuck arguing in circles, she was the one who made the call and moved us forward. Elisa was our Organiser, chasing deadlines and making sure meetings actually happened and led somewhere. Emilio was the Maker, the one who built things rather than just discussed them, from the service structure to the landing page. Matteo was our Consultant, mostly by disagreeing with us: he pushed back on assumptions and flagged risks we would have otherwise missed. Alessia was the Artist, responsible for how ComplAI looks, and also the one most likely to suggest we try something weirder. Arianna was the Helper and Enthusiast: she provided essential support where it was needed and kept the group's mood up when things got stressful.
